Hard irregular prostate nodule, Elevated PSA, Insidious lower urinary tract symptoms, Aching bone pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower back pain and sudden inability to pass urine, requiring immediate assessment for malignant spinal cord compression or acute urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute urinary retention superimposed on unremitting, severe aching bone pain in the lower back and pelvis. Urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally mandated to perform an immediate physical and neurological examination. Delaying this assessment until regular in-hours consultation posed an unacceptable risk of permanent paraplegia from spinal cord compression or irreversible acute kidney injury from obstruction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have not been able to empty my bladder for two days, and my lower back feels like it is being crushed in a vice.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of progressive lower urinary tract symptoms over the past year, including severe hesitancy and a weak urinary stream. Over the past month, they developed a deep, unremitting aching bone pain in their lower back and pelvis, which acutely escalated tonight. Concurrent with the bone pain, they experienced a complete inability to void. The patient reports unintentional significant weight loss of eight kilograms and profound anorexia in recent weeks. They also note bilateral lower extremity oedema.
Functional Status: Acutely non-ambulatory due to severe back pain and physical distress from urinary retention.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; living alone and unable to safely manage current acute pain and retention.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Cachectic, pale, appearing chronically unwell and in acute, severe physical distress.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Tense, dull, and exquisitely tender distended bladder palpable halfway to the umbilicus. Genitourinary: Digital rectal examination reveals a hard, irregular, asymmetrical, and nodular prostate gland fixed to surrounding tissues. Neurological: Lower limb power is intact, but testing is limited by severe bone pain; reflexes are normal. Extremities: Bilateral lower extremity oedema present. Expected investigation results include an elevated serum prostate-specific antigen and identification of malignant acinar adenocarcinoma on a future transrectal ultrasound-guided biopsy.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of profound lethargy and neglect in the home environment; uneaten meals present.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular analgesia prescribed; patient has been taking over-the-counter paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Prostate Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Urinary Retention and Skeletal Metastases)
Differentials:

Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary solitary cause due to the explicit presence of a hard, asymmetrical, nodular prostate on examination and concurrent metastatic bone pain.

Osteoporotic Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole diagnosis due to the concurrent severe lower urinary tract symptoms, massive urinary retention, and abnormal prostate examination.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely afebrile presentation and the insidious, consumptive history of significant weight loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical picture of acute urinary retention, severe unremitting lumbar bone pain, cachexia, and a hard, nodular prostate unequivocally points to advanced, metastatic prostate cancer. This represents an acute urological and oncological crisis. Urgent physical evaluation in the home was essential to definitively diagnose the mechanical obstruction, immediately drain the bladder to prevent irreversible acute kidney injury, and initiate high-dose analgesia for the metastatic bone pain. Attempting to delay this assessment until regular daytime general practice hours would constitute severe clinical negligence. This complex, high-acuity presentation strictly fulfills the criteria for an urgent MBS after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate insertion of a 16 French indwelling urethral catheter, draining 950mL of residual urine and providing instant relief of the suprapubic distress. Administered subcutaneous Morphine 5mg stat to manage the severe metastatic bone pain. Arranged urgent fast-tracked urology and oncology referrals. Ordered comprehensive morning bloods including UECs, FBC, Calcium, and PSA.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate accurate pain tracking prior to their specialist appointments, the patient is instructed to use a simple 1-to-10 pain scale diary every four hours. By documenting the exact time the pain begins to escalate, they can proactively take their prescribed breakthrough analgesia before the pain becomes unmanageable, reinforcing the habit of staying ahead of the pain cycle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Domiciliary nursing referral generated for daily catheter care and medication management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Leg drainage bag and overnight high-capacity bag provided.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to review renal function blood tests and ensure pain control is adequate pending specialist review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 is required if the patient develops sudden numbness or weakness in their legs, loss of bowel control, or if the catheter completely stops draining urine.

